<section class="section" id="about">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="about__grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
        <h2>Institutions, technology, and evidence</h2>
        <p>I am a Professor of International Political Economy in the
          <a href="{{ site.departmentUrl }}">Department of Politics and International Relations</a> and a Fellow of
          <a href="{{ site.collegeUrl }}">St John's College</a> at the University of Oxford. I am also Director of the
          <a href="{{ site.centreUrl }}">Centre for Technology and Society</a>, which studies the
          <span class="hl">social and economic consequences of emerging technologies</span>.</p>
        <p>I work on how <span class="hl">new technologies</span> redistribute
          <span class="hl--coral">political power</span>, why
          <span class="hl">international cooperation</span> succeeds or fails, and what drives
          <span class="hl--coral">economic development</span>. My book
          <em><a href="{{ '/book/' | url }}">Making International Institutions Work</a></em> examines why some
          international institutions perform better than others, drawing on the most
          comprehensive dataset on the topic. I also have methodological interests in machine
          learning and the analysis of missing data, and release the datasets and software
          behind my research openly.</p>
        <p>I received my PhD in Government from Harvard University and my BA in Philosophy,
          Politics, and Economics from the University of Oxford. Before beginning my PhD, I
          worked as an economist at the Bank of England and an editorial writer at the
          <em>Financial Times</em>.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Affiliations</span>
        <ul class="chips">
          <li class="chip"><span class="chip__k">Dept</span><span><a href="{{ site.departmentUrl }}">Politics &amp; International Relations</a></span></li>
          <li class="chip"><span class="chip__k">College</span><span><a href="{{ site.collegeUrl }}">St John's College</a></span></li>
          <li class="chip"><span class="chip__k">Centre</span><span><a href="{{ site.centreUrl }}">Director, Centre for Technology and Society</a></span></li>
          <li class="chip"><span class="chip__k">Previously</span><span>London School of Economics &middot; Financial Times &middot; Bank of England</span></li>
          <li class="chip"><span class="chip__k">Education</span><span>PhD Harvard University &middot; BA University of Oxford</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="themes">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Research themes</span>
      <h2>Four strands of work</h2>
      <p>Distinct questions, but a shared concern with what the evidence can and cannot support.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="themes">
      <article class="theme">
        <span class="theme__n">Strand I</span>
        <h3>Technological change</h3>
        <p>How AI and other emerging technologies are reshaping labour markets, redistributing
          economic and political power, and reordering the politics that follows from both.
          Much of this work now runs through the Centre for Technology and Society.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="theme">
        <span class="theme__n">Strand II</span>
        <h3>International cooperation</h3>
        <p>Why some international institutions perform better than others, how they are held to
          account, and what makes bureaucratic autonomy possible in a world of states pursuing
          narrow interests.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="theme">
        <span class="theme__n">Strand III</span>
        <h3>Economic development</h3>
        <p>What makes development finance work — the effectiveness of aid projects, the
          transparency of the agencies that run them, and the long-run historical forces that
          shape where prosperity takes hold.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="theme">
        <span class="theme__n">Methods</span>
        <h3>Missing data and machine learning</h3>
        <p>Techniques for drawing valid inferences from incomplete data, including deep-learning
          approaches to multiple imputation and tests for conditional independence — released as
          open-source packages for R and Python.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--navy" id="cts">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="cts">
      <div>
        <img class="cts__lockup" src="{{ '/assets/img/cts-lockup-white.svg' | url }}"
             alt="Centre for Technology and Society (CTS)" width="1060" height="250">
        <span class="eyebrow">Centre for Technology and Society</span>
        <h2>Understanding how technology is remaking society</h2>
        <p>I am Director of the <strong>Centre for Technology and Society</strong> at the
          University of Oxford, part of the Oxford Martin AI Governance Initiative. The Centre
          produces <span class="hl">evidence-first research</span> on how emerging technologies
          are reshaping work, power, and society &mdash; concentrating where the evidence is
          thinnest and the stakes are highest.</p>
        <p>Its two primary interests are the labour market implications of AI and the
          dispersion of power among firms, states, and citizens.</p>
        <div class="hero__actions" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="{{ site.centreUrl }}">Visit the Centre &rarr;</a>
          <a class="btn btn--outline" href="{{ site.centreUrl }}working-papers/">Working Paper Series &rarr;</a>
